WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:05.480
Once you've got your JCL written,

2
00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:09.700
chances are you actually want to submit it and make it run.

3
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:11.620
As you've probably picked up on by now,

4
00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:13.460
when there's something you want to do,

5
00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:15.920
there is usually like eight ways to do it.

6
00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:17.920
So I'm gonna show you two ways

7
00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:21.060
of submitting your JCL to JES.

8
00:00:21.060 --> 00:00:26.060
To begin, we're gonna open up option two of ISPF for edit.

9
00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:27.800
That will bring us to this screen

10
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:29.660
and you just specify the dataset,

11
00:00:29.660 --> 00:00:33.100
either using the ISPF library fields up there

12
00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:35.660
or the one below it labeled other partitions,

13
00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:40.380
sequential or VSAM dataset or zos-unix file.

14
00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:43.620
That will bring up this listing of dataset members.

15
00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:48.540
Place an S next to the one you want to select and hit enter.

16
00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:51.380
TSO will come back and pop up a little message saying,

17
00:00:51.380 --> 00:00:53.380
got it, I have submitted your job.

18
00:00:53.380 --> 00:00:55.780
Here's the job number I gave it.

19
00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:58.780
Now I said there's more than one way to do things.

20
00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:02.860
So I'm gonna show you another way of submitting JCL to JES.

21
00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:04.660
If you're at a TSO prompt,

22
00:01:04.660 --> 00:01:06.780
you can submit a job from here too.

23
00:01:06.780 --> 00:01:11.260
Just type TSO submit followed by the dataset member

24
00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:13.820
spelled out as the dataset name

25
00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:16.580
and then the specified member in parentheses.

26
00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:18.520
The dataset and dataset member

27
00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:20.940
should be within single quotes.

28
00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.860
It sounds a lot more complicated when I say it.

29
00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:26.100
Just look at how it's spelled out here.

30
00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:29.420
But when you put the TSO at the beginning of a statement,

31
00:01:29.420 --> 00:01:31.840
you're issuing a TSO command.

32
00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:33.640
So if you have some JCL

33
00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:34.960
and you don't even have to look at it,

34
00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:38.340
you just know you wanna run it, that's how you do that.

35
00:01:38.340 --> 00:01:40.240
To look at the output of that job,

36
00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:44.480
head into SDSF from the primary ISPF menu,

37
00:01:44.480 --> 00:01:49.080
then go into option H because we want to look at held jobs.

38
00:01:49.080 --> 00:01:52.200
As you can see, there's a lot you can do in here.

39
00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.700
All in due time, all in due time.

40
00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:56.660
When we hit enter for the held jobs,

41
00:01:56.660 --> 00:01:58.360
we get a listing of output

42
00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:00.560
and we can do a number of things with it.

43
00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:02.640
Right now, we want to display that output.

44
00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:06.680
So we'll place an S next to it and hit enter.

45
00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:09.920
That will bring up all of the output from that job.

46
00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:12.080
As I said, there's a lot you can do in here

47
00:02:12.080 --> 00:02:13.040
with other commands,

48
00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:16.680
but the most common commands are probably S for display,

49
00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:21.480
C for cancel, P for purge, and X for print.

50
00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:22.840
When you do view the output,

51
00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:25.640
first thing you'll notice is it is tremendously verbose

52
00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:27.760
and we're not gonna get into that here.

53
00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.260
I mean, pretty much every piece of information

54
00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:33.180
regarding the execution of that job is in here

55
00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:35.220
and it would take a whole lot of time

56
00:02:35.220 --> 00:02:36.780
to fully explore that output.

57
00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.980
So we'll just stop right here and be happy

58
00:02:38.980 --> 00:02:40.800
that we know how to build JCL,

59
00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:43.220
submit it, and manage the output.

60
00:02:43.220 --> 00:02:44.160
Stay tuned though.

61
00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.160
Later on, I'm gonna show you another way

62
00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:47.520
to look at system output.
